The prefix flag is set where every prefix operator passes, not where only some do.

WHAT THIS CLOSES, AND A CORRECTION TO EXPERIMENT-zzc.

zzc fixed `--p->n`, which added the member offset to the ADDRESS of the pointer
variable instead of to its value. It left `--s.n` on a struct variable failing,
and its own preamble says:

    "`--s.n` on a LOCAL STRUCT is a DIFFERENT bug ... It is a different site
    with a different cause, and the first attempt at a joint fix -- suppressing
    the dot site's final load under the same flag -- did NOT close it. That is
    evidence, not an assumption."

Both claims are wrong, and the second one is the worse of the two.

IT IS THE SAME CAUSE. zzc set prefix_lvalue_target inside
primary_expr_variable, beside the code that decides whether to load the
variable. A STRUCT variable returns from that function long before reaching
that line, so for `--s.n` the flag was never set and the dot site had nothing
to read. Proved by emitting a marker there: reached twice for `--p->len`, never
once for `--s.len`.

AND THE "EVIDENCE" WAS A NUMBER THE TOOL HAD ALREADY DISOWNED. The joint fix
was judged failed on a difftest bitmask of 139. difftest prints, for exactly
this situation:

    BITMASK 139 IS OUT OF RANGE -- fix the case to stay under 128

because an exit code above 127 is indistinguishable from a signal at the shell.
The case had nine probes summing to 511. The number could not mean what it was
read to mean, the tool said so on the same line, and a wrong conclusion was
written into a patch preamble as a finding.

That is the failure mode `MICRO-C.md` already has a section for -- "the tool
answered confidently in a case it could not distinguish" -- committed by the
person who had just read that section. The raw data was correct. The sentence
underneath it was not.

THE FIX IS ONE LINE MOVED. prefix_lvalue_target is now set in the prefix
handler in primary_expr: the single point every prefix ++/-- passes through,
before the operand is parsed at all. The arrow site keeps its own line in
primary_expr_variable, because it must ALSO force the pointer to be loaded, and
that is a genuinely different question from "who is the lvalue".

postfix_expr_dot gains the guard beside the postfix operator it already knew
about -- a POSTFIX operator sits where a lookahead can see it, a PREFIX one
does not, which is the whole shape of this class.

WHETHER IT MATTERS FOR tcc: YES, unlike the three patches before it.

    ++indexsym.c;                          tccgen.c:8086
    while (str.str[--str.len] != ',')      tccpp.c:3101

The second is on the token path, and it is the shape case 61 probes: the
decremented member used immediately as an index. zzc's arrow half was on tcc's
path too -- 18 sites across the unit -- which is worth stating because zzd, zze
and zzf were not, and a run of green cases should not be allowed to imply
progress on the subject.

MEASURED

    difftest      58 -> 60 pass, 3 known gaps -> 1, both architectures
    corpus        423 of 426 on aarch64, unchanged
    vocabulary    clean on five architectures
    tcc           compiles, links and runs as before; the fault has NOT moved

    Cases 57 and 61 were RED before this patch and are GREEN after, and the
    suite reports the transition rather than silently going quiet:
        KNOWN GAP NOW PASSES -- close it or fix the case

THE LAST REMAINING KNOWN GAP is case 44, `&((*p)->m)`. It does not occur
anywhere in tcc -- checked, not assumed -- so it is left open deliberately.

